Title: "Rings and Fields"
Id: "832946c5-ae2c-47d4-b445-3ded9c71199c"


Text: "While groups formalize symmetries through a single invertible operation, the
       richness of classical arithmetic and geometry requires the simultaneous presence
       of two interacting operations: addition and multiplication.
       
       A *ring* unites these operations: addition forms an abelian group (permitting
       arbitrary translations and subtractions), multiplication forms a monoid
       (providing composition and an identity), and the two operations are bound
       together by the *distributive laws*:
       $$ a \cdot (b + c) = a \cdot b + a \cdot c \quad \text{and} \quad (a + b) \cdot c = a \cdot c + b \cdot c. $$
       The distributive property ensures that multiplication behaves as an endomorphism
       of the additive group. Conceived through the pioneering work of Richard Dedekind,
       David Hilbert, and Emmy Noether, rings provide the overarching framework for
       integers, polynomials, matrices, and algebraic functions."
Id: "7c74e436-b04f-4233-8161-ed9e8ffc13a7"


SectionTitle: "Rings"
Id: "0297b76e-dfa0-495e-a363-8f9f27d2ded3"


[\ring]
Declares: R ::= (X, +, *, 0, 1)
specifies:
. R is \(abelian)::group via (X, +, 0)
. R is \monoid via (X, *, 1)
satisfies:
. [distributive]
  forAll: a, b, c "in" X
  then:
  . a * (b + c) = (a * b) + (a * c)
  . (a + b) * c = (a * c) + (b * c)
Enables:
. capability: x_ "in" R :<->: x_ is \ring.element:of{R}
  written: "x_? \in R?"
Documented:
. called: "ring"
. description: "A ring $(R, +, *, 0, 1)$ consists of an abelian addition group,
                an associative multiplication with identity $1$, and two-sided
                distributivity."
Id: "5ca5fdca-692d-4f04-8420-aa01c909955d"


[\ring.element:of{R ::= (X, +, *, 0, 1)}]
Declares: x "in" X
when: R is \ring
Enables:
. capability: x_ [+] y_ :=> x_ |R.+| y_
  written: "x_? +? y_?"
. capability: x_ [*] y_ :=> x_ |R.*| y_
  written: "x_? *? y_?"
Documented:
. called: "element of ring $R?$"
. description: "An element of a ring $R$ is a member of its carrier set $X$."
Id: "7e51f2cf-8568-4819-af9d-80949b74d813"


Text: "A ring is *commutative* when its multiplication satisfies $a * b = b * a$
       for all elements. Commutative rings serve as the algebraic foundation of
       algebraic geometry, where geometric shapes (varieties and schemes) are
       studied through their commutative rings of polynomial or regular
       functions.

       In contrast, non-commutative rings—such as matrix rings $M_n(R)$,
       endomorphism rings $\operatorname{End}(V)$, and differential
       operators—arise naturally as transformation algebras across linear
       algebra and quantum physics."
Id: "64b4ac6a-9629-4aa7-89e2-9f0ea27e9a48"


[\(commutative)::ring]
Refines: R ::= (X, +, *, 0, 1)
specifies: * is \(commutative)::binary.operation:on{X}
Documented:
. adjective: "commutative"
. description: "A commutative ring is a ring in which $x * y = y * x$ for all
                elements."
Id: "2ddbe95a-149f-4596-8496-b214741d64a4"


SectionTitle: "Fields"
Id: "0c54d423-2239-4bcc-9398-cff3fa696e7c"


Text: "A *field* is a non-trivial commutative ring in which every nonzero
       element has a multiplicative inverse: the set of nonzero elements
       $F^\times = F \setminus \{0\}$ forms an abelian group under
       multiplication.

       Fields represent the optimal arithmetic environment: addition,
       subtraction, multiplication, and division (by nonzero elements) are
       universally permitted and obey all classical algebraic symmetries. Fields
       provide the scalar domain for linear algebra and vector spaces, and their
       algebraic extensions are the focus of Galois theory."
Id: "93eece57-714d-474a-8c52-840bbb3f6621"


[\field]
Declares: F ::= (X, +, *, 0, 1)
specifies: F is \(commutative)::ring via (X, +, *, 0, 1)
satisfies:
. 0 != 1
. (X \.set.difference./ \singleton.set:of{0}, *, 1) is? \(abielan)::group
Enables:
. capability: x_ "in" F :<->: x_ is \field.element:of{F}
  written: "x_? \in F?"
. view:
  as: G := (X \.set.difference./ \singleton.set:of{0}, *, 1) is \(abelian)::group
Documented:
. called: "field"
. description: "A field is a commutative ring $(F, +, *, 0, 1)$ with $0 \neq 1$
                in which every non-zero element has a multiplicative inverse."
Id: "53a76a9b-97d9-4c4d-b6c6-cc00983cfcb2"


[\field.element:of{F ::= (X, +, *, 0, 1)}]
Declares: x is \ring.element:of{F}
when: F is \field
Documented:
. called: "element of field $F?$"
. description: "An element of a field $F$ is an element of its underlying ring."
Id: "1961828f-5a83-4c78-b8c9-a91c2e512d50"


SectionTitle: "Ideals, Quotients, and Characteristic"
Id: "ca7d140b-b005-46b1-b844-511a2293e65d"


[\ideal:of{R ::= (X, +, *, 0, 1)}]
Declares: I \:subset:/ X
when: R is \ring
satisfies:
. 0 "in" I
. forAll: x, y "in" I
  then: x + y "in" I
. forAll: x "in" I
  then:
  . exists: y "in" I
    suchThat: x + y = 0
. forAll:
  . r "in" R
  . i "in" I
  then:
  . r * i "in" I
  . i * r "in" I
Documented:
. called: "ideal of $R?$"
. description: "An ideal is an additive subgroup closed under multiplication by
                arbitrary ring elements."
Id: "999d97fa-3609-4900-a19d-ab1f3345ca54"


[\(zero.divisor)::ring.element:of{R ::= (X, +, *, 0, 1)}]
Refines: a
when: R is \(commutative)::ring
satisfies:
. a != 0
. exists: b "in" R
  suchThat:
  . b != 0
  . a * b = 0
Documented:
. adjective: "zero divisor"
Id: "cff0d878-33b9-4552-93c2-cee960162e9e"


[\integral.domain]
Declares: D ::= (X, +, *, 0, 1)
specifies: D is \(commutative)::ring via (X, +, *, 0, 1)
satisfies:
. 0 != 1
. forAll: a, b "in" D
  then: (. a * b = 0 .) \.implies./ (. a = 0 \.or./ b = 0 .)
Documented:
. called: "integral domain"
. description: "A nontrivial commutative ring without zero divisors."
Id: "be91bf4c-366f-430f-bff8-6f9c7225736d"


TextDefinition: "The *characteristic* $\operatorname{char}(R)$ of a ring with
                 identity is the least positive integer $n$ such that
                 $n \cdot 1 = 0$, if such an integer exists, and is $0$
                 otherwise.

                 Equivalently, the characteristic is the unique non-negative
                 generator of the kernel of the canonical ring homomorphism
                 $\chi : \mathbb{Z} \to R$ defined by $\chi(m) = m \cdot 1$.

                 In an integral domain $D$, if $n \cdot 1 = 0$ with
                 $n = a \cdot b$, then $(a \cdot 1) \cdot (b \cdot 1) = 0$
                 forces $a \cdot 1 = 0$ or $b \cdot 1 = 0$. Hence the
                 characteristic of an integral domain (and therefore of any
                 field) is either $0$ or a prime number $p$. Consequently, every
                 field contains a unique minimal prime subfield: either the
                 rational field $\mathbb{Q}$ (if $\operatorname{char}(F) = 0$)
                 or the finite Galois field
                 $\mathbb{F}_p = \mathbb{Z}/p\mathbb{Z}$ (if
                 $\operatorname{char}(F) = p$)."
Id: "e5fe50b7-6f61-4ad4-85e8-0fec0260b2cf"


TextTheorem: "Let $R$ be a commutative ring and $I \subsetneq R$ a proper ideal.
              The quotient ring $R/I$ reflects the algebraic tightness of $I$:
              1. $R/I$ is an integral domain if and only if $I$ is a *prime
                 ideal* (meaning
                 $a \cdot b \in I \implies a \in I \text{ or } b \in I$).
              2. $R/I$ is a field if and only if $I$ is a *maximal ideal*
                 (meaning there is no ideal $J$ strictly between $I$ and $R$).

              *Proof Sketch of (2)*: If $I$ is maximal and $a + I \neq 0 + I$ in
              $R/I$, then $a \notin I$. The ideal generated by $I$ and $a$,
              $I + Ra$, strictly contains $I$, so by maximality $I + Ra = R$.
              Since $1 \in R$, there exist $i \in I$ and $r \in R$ such that
              $i + ra = 1$. In the quotient ring, $(r + I)(a + I) = 1 + I$,
              proving that $a + I$ has a multiplicative inverse. Conversely, if
              $R/I$ is a field, its only ideals are $(0)$ and $R/I$, so by the
              Lattice Isomorphism Theorem, no intermediate ideals exist between
              $I$ and $R$.

              Since every field is an integral domain, every maximal ideal is
              prime."
Id: "81f097c0-c0ee-4819-8304-7f13856e2551"
